<p class="lead">Spring is our home base, and few places in North Houston offer a wider range of properties to light. Within a short drive you can go from the historic, small-town character of Old Town Spring to wooded subdivisions under tall pines, newer master-planned neighborhoods, and larger lots with long driveways and room to breathe. Spring Landscape Lighting designs each system around the property in front of us, because a plan that suits one of these homes rarely suits the next.</p>

<h2>One town, many kinds of lots</h2>
<p>The variety is what makes lighting in Spring interesting. A few patterns we see often:</p>
<div class="grid-cards">
  <div class="mini-card"><h3>Older and character homes</h3><p>In and around Old Town Spring, many homes and buildings have porches, wood siding and cottage proportions. These usually want warm, low-key light that respects the scale rather than dramatic uplighting.</p></div>
  <div class="mini-card"><h3>Wooded subdivisions</h3><p>Mature pines and oaks shade many established neighborhoods. Trees become the main feature at night, and moonlighting can light lawns and walks naturally.</p></div>
  <div class="mini-card"><h3>Newer brick and stone homes</h3><p>Two-story elevations with stone entries and tall gables benefit from layered <a href="/services/architectural-lighting/">architectural lighting</a> balanced with the young landscape.</p></div>
  <div class="mini-card"><h3>Larger lots</h3><p>Deep setbacks and long drives call for selective <a href="/services/pathway-driveway-lighting/">driveway and path lighting</a>, not a fixture every few feet.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>What Spring weather asks of a system</h2>
<p>Lighting here has to handle hot, humid summers, heavy downpours, irrigation running several times a week, and the occasional tropical storm. Practically, that means sealed waterproof connections instead of tape, fixtures made of brass, copper or quality aluminum, transformers mounted above standing water, and wire buried deep enough to survive edging and aeration. Landscapes also grow fast. St. Augustine creeps over walk edges and foundation shrubs can hide a fixture in a single season, so we place lights with future growth in mind.</p>

<div class="callout"><strong>Tip:</strong> Sunset in Spring shifts by about two hours between winter and summer. An astronomical timer follows it automatically. Our <a href="/tools/sunset-timer-planner/">sunset and timer planner</a> shows the month-by-month times.</div>

<h2>Where to start</h2>
<p>Most homeowners begin with the front of the house and the walk to the door, then add trees and the backyard over time. A well-planned system makes that easy: a transformer sized with room to grow, zones that can be extended, and a consistent color temperature so new fixtures match the old. If you already have an older halogen system, a <a href="/services/landscape-lighting-repair/">repair or LED upgrade</a> is often the most cost-effective first step.</p>
<p>For a sense of budget, our article on <a href="/landscape-lighting-cost-spring-tx/">landscape lighting cost in Spring, TX</a> explains what drives pricing. When you are ready, Spring Landscape Lighting can walk the property with you and put together a plan.</p>

